([nil nil ((#("
 <p> To ensure that robustness and controllability is not lost when embracing black-box neural advances~(e.g. foundation models), domain-specific conversational systems could benefit from adhering to interpretable modularized designs, by factorizing tasks into logically abstracted or theory-grounded
        sub-modules according to our desired controllability. My research addresses the following 
challenges:
       </p>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Modularized NLP.</b> To connect each module and make a coherent
        system, my research studies <b>deep structured
          prediction</b> for extracting symbolic representations from text (<a
        href=\"https:
                //www.proquest.com/docview/2777357718?
                        pq-origsite=gscholar&fromopenview=true\">[Dissertation'22]
      </a>, <a href=\"https://aclanthology.org/K19-2013/\">[CoNLL'19]</a>, <a
               href=\"https://aclanthology.org/P19-1563/\">[ACL'19]</a>, <a
               href=\"https://aclanthology.org/2021.naacl-main.62/\">[NAACL'21]
      </a>, <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2302.12944\">[IWSDS'23]</a>) and
      <b>controllable generation</b> with augmented-memory, symbolic or neural-symbolic constraints (<a href=\"http:
                                                       //workshop.colips.org/dstc7/papers/13.pdf\">[DSTC7'19]</a>,<a
                       href=\"https://aclanthology.org/P19-1192/\">[ACL'19]</a>, <a href=\"https://aclanthology.org/2023.bea-1.47/\">[BEA'23]</a>).</li>
      <li><b>Learning with Inductive Biases.</b> Beyond the expensive supervised
        data in many domains~(e.g., psychotherapy, teaching, tutoring, small-group
      collaboration), we investigate learning with domain-specific knowledges as inductive biases, such as graph-based parsing via anchoring-based factorization<a href=\"https://aclanthology.org/K19-2013/\">[CoNLL'19]</a>,  
      database workload characterization via self-supervised learning <a href=\"https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.14778/3503585.3503600\">[VLDB'22]</a>, zero-shot dialogue state tracking via description-driven learning and supplementary
      pretraining <a href=\"https://aclanthology.org/2021.naacl-main.62/\">[NAACL'21]</a>.</li>
    <li><b>Deployment in Real-world Scenarios.</b> Unexpected, complex
        dialogue scenarios can potentially span infinitely many topics, 
        states and environmental settings <a href=\"https:
          //aclanthology.org/2023.acl-short.156\">[ACL'23]</a>. We study 
        robustness issues when deploying dialogue system in our daily life, such as in-classroom AI partner <a href=\"https://mlciv.com/papers/cao-jigsaw23.pdf\">[AIAIC'23]</a>, noisy speech <a href=\"https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1145/3565472.3595606\">[UMAP'23]</a>, multi-party, multi-modal dynamics<a href=\"https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1145/3565472.3595606\">[IWSDS'23]</a>,  and conversational simulation.</li>
    </ul>

